# encoding/json (step 9.11, ADR-102): Go's package, with the stack guard at the end of this file. The notes are the differences that come from the machine.

@note Nesting depth. Go's decoder refuses a document nested over 10000 deep before it builds anything (`Valid` and `Unmarshal` into a `RawMessage` do exactly that here, in every build), but building the value recurses on the C++ stack, which is fixed (8 MiB for the main thread and for each goroutine, divergence D24), so what a build can nest below 10000 depends on the compiler and the mode: see the stack guard note below, which turns the rest into an error.

@note Reference cycles. `Marshal` finds a cycle of pointers, maps or slices as Go does (an error after 1000 levels), but a value that is a cycle is never freed (D1), like any cyclic value. A cycle through types that have `#Derive(JSON)` is found the same way (a patch repeats `ptrEncoder`'s detection for them).

@note Types with `#Derive(JSON)`. They get two methods besides `MarshalJSON` and `UnmarshalJSON` (`GoCPPJSONPlain`, `GoCPPJSONPlainPtr`: the value, and its address, as a struct of the same fields with no methods), and the decoder (`indirect`) and the encoder (`marshalerEncoder`, `addrMarshalerEncoder`) use them to decode and encode the value as the struct it stands for, without calling the two methods: those write each level to a buffer and scan it again, which makes a nested value cost the square of its depth (ADR-102). The options of the `Decoder` and the `Encoder` reach such a value, as they reach a struct without methods.

@note Speed. About 3 to 7 times Go's time at `-O2`, and the stack guard costs less than 2% (ADR-102 has the numbers): the encoder and the decoder are reflection and interface heavy, and a counted pointer or slice costs an atomic operation where Go's collector costs none.

# ---- The stack guard (step 9.11 review): a nesting that the C++ stack cannot hold is an error, not a crash ----

@note Stack guard. Go's goroutine stack grows to 1 GB, so its decoder and encoder recurse as deep as the data goes (the decoder refuses documents nested over 10000 deep; the encoder has no limit). gocpp's stacks are fixed (8 MiB for the main thread and for each goroutine, D24) and a frame of the reflective code is several KB at `-O0`, so a hostile document or a deep value could end the program with `fatal error: stack overflow`. The patches ask the runtime how much stack is left (`stackRoom`: the bytes between the stack pointer and a reserve of 1/16 of the stack, 128 to 512 KiB, below which nothing else needs to run) at every level of the recursion and give an error when there is none: the decoder (`value`, `valueInterface`) a `*SyntaxError` "exceeded max depth (...)", the encoder (`ptrEncoder`, `sliceEncoder`, `mapEncoder`, `interfaceEncoder` and the four that call a `MarshalJSON` or `MarshalText` method) an `*UnsupportedValueError` "exceeded max depth (...)". Go's own limit of 10000 for the decoder stays and is checked first by the scanner. What a build can nest depends on the compiler and the mode: documents nested over 10000 deep are always a clean error; below that, with the 8 MiB stack, `Unmarshal` into `any` reaches about 9100 (g++ -O0), 6800 (clang++ -O0), 4700 (-asan) and 10000 (-release), into a recursive struct about 1400, 1000, 680 and 2200 to 4100, and `Marshal` of a linked list about 3200, 2600, 1600 and 5100 to 7000 (measured before the guard; the reserve takes about 6% of these, and the exact figure is the stack's). The guard costs one call into the runtime for each value that can recurse.

@file decode.go
@why Go's goroutine stacks grow; gocpp's are fixed: stackRoom asks the runtime how much is left (see the note), and a nesting that does not fit is an error.
@replace
func (d *decodeState) unmarshal(v any) error {
	rv := reflect.ValueOf(v)
@with
// stackRoom returns how many bytes of this goroutine's stack can still be used before the reserve that the runtime keeps; it is negative when the
// recursion must stop. gocpp's runtime provides it.
func stackRoom() int64

// errTooDeep is what the decoder and the encoder report when the stack has no room for one more level of nesting.
const errTooDeep = "exceeded max depth (the value is nested too deep for the stack)"

// tooDeepPanic is what valueInterface (which has no error result) panics with; unmarshal turns it into the error.
type tooDeepPanic struct{}

// derivedPlainPtr is implemented by the types that have #Derive(JSON): the method returns the address of the value as a pointer to a struct type with the
// same fields and no methods of its own (the type that the generated MarshalJSON and UnmarshalJSON encode and decode). The encoder and the decoder use it to
// work on that struct directly, as they do for a struct without methods: nothing is written to a buffer and scanned again at each level, so a deeply nested
// value costs what a plain one costs instead of the square of its depth.
type derivedPlainPtr interface{ GoCPPJSONPlainPtr() any }

func (d *decodeState) unmarshal(v any) (err error) {
	defer func() {
		if r := recover(); r != nil {
			if _, ok := r.(tooDeepPanic); ok {
				err = &SyntaxError{errTooDeep, int64(d.off)}
				return
			}
			panic(r)
		}
	}()
	rv := reflect.ValueOf(v)
@end

@why err is a result of unmarshal now (the recover sets it): it is assigned, not declared.
@replace
	err := d.value(rv)
	if err != nil {
		return d.addErrorContext(err)
	}
	return d.savedError
@with
	err = d.value(rv)
	if err != nil {
		return d.addErrorContext(err)
	}
	return d.savedError
@end

@why The recursion value -> object/array -> value must stop when the stack is full.
@replace
func (d *decodeState) value(v reflect.Value) error {
	switch d.opcode {
@with
func (d *decodeState) value(v reflect.Value) error {
	if stackRoom() < 0 {
		return &SyntaxError{errTooDeep, int64(d.off)}
	}
	switch d.opcode {
@end

@why The recursion valueInterface -> arrayInterface/objectInterface -> valueInterface must stop when the stack is full.
@replace
func (d *decodeState) valueInterface() (val any) {
	switch d.opcode {
@with
func (d *decodeState) valueInterface() (val any) {
	if stackRoom() < 0 {
		panic(tooDeepPanic{})
	}
	switch d.opcode {
@end

@why A type with #Derive(JSON) is decoded as the struct it stands for, without calling its UnmarshalJSON (which would scan the document again at each level).
@replace
			if u, ok := v.Interface().(Unmarshaler); ok {
				return u, nil, reflect.Value{}
			}
@with
			if u, ok := v.Interface().(Unmarshaler); ok {
				if dp, ok := u.(derivedPlainPtr); ok && derivedPlainType(v.Type().Elem()) != nil {
					// The struct stands for the type: go on with a pointer to it, which finds the methods that it has by promotion (an embedded time.Time, say).
					v = reflect.ValueOf(dp.GoCPPJSONPlainPtr())
					haveAddr = false
					continue
				}
				return u, nil, reflect.Value{}
			}
@end

@file encode.go
@why The encoders that recurse (pointers, slices, maps, interfaces) must stop when the stack is full.
@replace
